2026-09-11T23:00:00.000Z note implemented by hand on the maintainer's instruction to fast-track, as Q-0118 was — and the risk profile was argued BEFORE agreeing rather than after: Q-0118's fifteen findings were overwhelmingly lifecycle and concurrency (leaked subscriptions, an unhandled rejection, a bind race, backpressure, shutdown ordering), and this surface is read-only over symbols already on the barrel, with no sockets, no state and no lifecycle.
2026-09-11T23:00:00.001Z note the prediction held, measured: THREE findings across two cross-vendor rounds, against six/five/four for Q-0118. Round 1: readRun's parsed document is "a cast, never a check" by its own JSDoc, so a hand-edited rollup that is not an array made `.map()` throw where nothing caught — one damaged file becoming a 500 with a Node stack; and RUN_HISTORY_ROOT was redeclared here rather than imported from @quorum/shared, which already owns that spelling. Round 2: the guard checked the CONTAINER and not the elements, so `[1,2,3]` merely moved the throw inside the map.
2026-09-11T23:00:00.002Z note the Q-0118 lesson was applied deliberately: every route got its test in the SAME pass rather than code first and gaps in review. It caught three wrong assumptions of mine before any reviewer saw them — lintFlowDirectory takes the flows directory rather than the harness directory, sortRuns falls back to run_id ascending only as a tiebreak, and the Q-0055 classifier test proved NOTHING until its fixture was a flow that parses and is then refused. A file that fails to parse leaves both classifiers agreeing, which is why the first version passed with the wrong one; mutating to `record.flow !== undefined` now fails by name.
2026-09-11T23:00:00.003Z note history is rooted at /history rather than /runs, decided here: POST /runs/:id/* already uses the host's live handle and a history id is <TICKET>-<n>, so two id spaces on one path shape would be a trap for a client and for whoever adds the next route. docs/04-architecture.md names "history" too.
